\p
\v 11 From¦94917 Troas¦94919 we¦94920 sailed directly to Samothrace¦94922, and¦94925 then on¦94924 to Neapolis the¦94924 following¦94927 day¦94924.
\v 12 From¦94933 there¦94933 we continued on to¦94936 Philippi¦94937 which¦94938 is¦94939 a \add Roman\add* colony¦94948 and¦94951 the capital city¦94947 of¦94943 the Macedonia¦94946 district¦94944 where we stayed¦94957 for several days¦94958.
\v 13 On¦94960 the¦94964 Rest¦94965 Days¦94965, we went¦94966 \add down\add* to the river¦94973 outside¦94967 the \add city\add* gate¦94969 where¦94974 we thought¦94978 people might be¦94981 praying, and¦94962 we spoke¦94984 to the¦94985 women¦94989 who had met there.
\v 14 One of them was Lydia¦94994 from Thyatira¦94998 who was a seller¦94995 of purple¦94995 goods\f + \fr 16:14 \ft It's possible that Lydia dyed fabric and sold it, or that she actually made and sold the purple dye itself.\f* and although she already worshipped¦94999 \nd God¦95002\nd*, the¦95006 \nd master¦95007\nd* opened¦95008 her¦95009 mind¦95010 and she readily accepted what Paul¦95016 was saying.
\v 15 So then Lydia and her¦95024 household were all \add ceremonially\add* immersed¦95019, and then she requested, “If¦95027 you¦95028 all¦95028 consider me¦95029 to be¦95034 faithful¦95030 to the¦95031 \nd master¦95033\nd*, come¦95035 and stay at my¦95041 place.” So she persuaded us¦95047.
\s1 Paul and Silas get thrown into prison
