\p
\v 16 While Paul¦96110 stayed in Athens¦96104 waiting for them¦96107, he was¦96111 upset in his¦96114 spirit¦96113 as he observed¦96118 how idolatrous¦96119 the¦96121 people were.
\v 17 So¦96125 while he stayed busy having discussions¦96123 with the¦96129 Jews¦96130 and¦96131 other worshippers¦96133 from the¦96127 meeting¦96128 hall¦96128, he also¦96131 spent time every¦96140 day¦96141 with anyone¦96124 who just happened¦96144 to¦96144 be¦96144 around in¦96126 the¦96137 marketplace¦96138.
\v 18 Some¦96145 of¦96148 the¦96148 Epicurean and¦96150 Stoic philosophers¦96153 enjoyed discussing things with¦96156 him, but¦96170 others¦96169 just¦96166 mocked him, “What¦96160 does this¦96166 babbler¦96165 want¦96162 to¦96167 talk about now?”
\p But¦96150 others said¦96159, “Seems that¦96166 he's¦96173 talking¦96159 about strange¦96171 demons¦96172,” because¦96176 he was¦96183 preaching¦96183 the good¦96183 message¦96183 about \nd Yeshua¦96178\nd* and coming back to life.
\v 19 So they¦96199 grabbed¦96185 him and brought¦96199 him to the¦96196 Aries¦96197 Hill¦96198 \add where the council sat\add* and asked¦96203, “Are¦96204 we¦96204 allowed to know¦96205 what¦96206 this¦96209 new¦96208 teaching¦96216 that¦96210 you're bringing is¦96216 about¦96192?
\v 20 It seems that you¦96221 have some¦96219 surprising¦96217 ideas for¦96218 our¦96228 ears¦96227, so we¦96229 want¦96235 to¦96225 hear what¦96233 it's all¦96233 about¦96228.”
\v 21 (Everyone¦96249 in¦96248 Athens and¦96242 even visitors¦96244 often took the¦96239 opportunity¦96251 either to explain or¦96255 to listen to any¦96258 new things.)
\p
\v 22 So¦96261 Paul¦96263 stood¦96260 up in¦96264 the middle of the gathering on the hill and¦96261 spoke, “Men¦96271 of Athens, I've noticed how¦96275 you¦96277 all¦96277 are very religious,
\v 23 because¦96280 as I was looking around and¦96281 observing¦96283 your¦96287 objects¦96286 of¦96286 worship, I even¦96289 discovered an¦96290 altar¦96290 that was inscribed¦96295, ‘To¦96296 the¦96284 unknown¦96296 \nd god¦96297\nd*.’ So¦96300 without knowing¦96301 it, you already worship this¦96303 \nd God¦96297\nd* that I'm¦96305 going to¦96307 tell you about¦96286.
\v 24 This¦96320 \nd God¦96310\nd* who created the¦96313 earth and everything in it¦96319 is¦96331 \nd Yahweh¦96326\nd* who's over heaven¦96321 and earth. He doesn't live in man-made temples¦96330
\v 25 nor¦96335 can he be repaired by¦96336 any human¦96338 hands¦96337 and he doesn't need to¦96351 be fed. \add On the contrary\add*, he's¦96346 the one who gives life¦96352 and breath¦96354 and who gives us everything that we need.
\v 26 From one¦96362 man¦96362 he¦96359 made¦96359 every¦96364 nationality¦96365 that lives across all¦96370 the¦96375 earth¦96376 and designated¦96377 their boundaries¦96384 and their futures.
\v 27 He made them so that they would try to¦96390 discover \nd God¦96393\nd*, and¦96404 if¦96397 they did search for¦96401 him they might¦96405 find¦96405 him as he's not¦96411 far¦96412 from¦96414 any one¦96415 of¦96418 us¦96418
\v 28 because¦96423
\q1 ‘We¦96424 live¦96424 in¦96420 him and are¦96426 moved¦96426 by him and exist through him.’
