\p
\v 5 When¦97438 the followers heard¦97437 this, they¦97440 were¦97440 immersed¦97440 \add in water again, this time proclaiming their faith\add* in \nd Yeshua¦97446\nd* the \nd master¦97445\nd*.
\v 6 Then¦97452 Paul¦97458 placed his hands¦97460 on¦97453 them \add and prayed\add* and the¦97466 holy¦97467 \nd spirit¦97465\nd* came¦97463 \add down\add* onto them and they¦97471 started speaking in other languages¦97475 and speaking messages from God.
\v 7 There were¦97478 about¦97483 twelve¦97484 men¦97482 in the¦97480 group.
\rem /s1 Paul Ministers in Ephesus
\p
\v 8 So¦97486 Paul started going to the \add ≈Jewish¦97491 meeting¦97491 hall¦97491\add* and for¦97496 the next three¦97498 months¦97497 he¦97495 boldly¦97495 discussed¦97499 and persuaded¦97501 them about¦97503 \nd God's¦97507\nd* kingdom¦97505.
\v 9 But¦97515 as some¦97510 of¦97517 them¦97530 refused to believe and became hardened¦97514 against his teaching and started telling¦97517 everyone¦97535 that the \add Yeshua\add* way¦97519 was evil¦97517, Paul stopped teaching in¦97535 the meeting hall and taught the¦97532 followers¦97533 at Turannos' school¦97540 instead.
\v 10 This¦97548 went on for¦97551 two¦97553 years¦97552 to¦97566 the point that¦97548 everyone¦97556 in¦97560 Asia¦97564 \add Minor\add*, whether Jews¦97575 or Greeks¦97579, heard¦97566 the¦97568 message¦97570 about¦97572 Yeshua.
\s1 The power of the master's message
\rem /s1 The Sons of Sceva
\p
\v 11 And¦97583 \nd God¦97589\nd* was¦97590 doing¦97590 incredible miracles¦97581 through Paul¦97594
\v 12 such that even¦97596 if handkerchiefs¦97606 or aprons¦97609 that had touched his¦97605 skin¦97604 were placed on top of¦97605 any person who was sick or troubled with demons, then they would recover and¦97610 the¦97614 evil¦97620 spirits¦97618 would leave them¦97613.
\v 13 But¦97626 some¦97627 of the Jewish exorcists¦97635 going¦97632 around¦97632 attempted¦97625 to use the¦97644 name¦97645 of Yeshua to drive out evil¦97643 spirits¦97641 by telling¦97649 them, “I am¦97652 imploring¦97652 you¦97653 all¦97653 by Yeshua that Paul¦97660 preaches about¦97646.”
\v 14 Seven¦97676 sons¦97677 of¦97668 the Jewish chief¦97674 priest¦97674, Sceva¦97670 were¦97664 doing¦97682 this¦97681.
\p
\v 15 But¦97726 the¦97718 evil¦97719 spirit¦97717 would answer¦97713 them¦97721 saying¦97720, “I know¦97725 \nd Yeshua¦97724\nd* and¦97726 I recognise¦97729 Paul¦97728, but¦97731 who¦97732 are¦97733 you¦97730?”
\p
\v 16 Then¦97734 the¦97741 man¦97742 who had the¦97750 evil¦97751 spirit¦97749 would jump on¦97735 them and beat them up so¦97762 they'd end up escaping¦97766 out¦97768 of¦97768 the house¦97770 naked¦97763 and wounded¦97765.
\v 17 When¦97773 everyone¦97776 in¦97782 Ephesus¦97784 heard about¦97798 this¦97772, both¦97778 Jews¦97777 and Greeks¦97780 were frightened, and the¦97796 name¦97797 of¦97798 \nd Yeshua¦97800\nd* the \nd master¦97799\nd* was¦97795 being¦97795 honoured.
\v 18 Many¦97801 of¦97804 the¦97804 people who believed¦97805 \add in Yeshua\add* came¦97807 and confessed¦97808 \add their sins\add* and wrong practices¦97812.
\v 19 Lots of the people who'd practiced¦97821 magic¦97820 brought¦97822 their¦97824 scrolls¦97825 and¦97815 burnt¦97826 them¦97826 in front¦97827 of the others. When¦97815 the values of all¦97828 of these were¦97826 added up¦97826, it was \add huge\add*—worth around 50,000 silver¦97836 \add coins\add*.
\v 20 So that was¦97853 how the¦97851 \nd master's¦97849\nd* message¦97852 had a powerful¦97841 effect and¦97854 won over many people.
\s1 The silversmiths start a ruckus in Ephesus
\rem /s1 The Riot in Ephesus
\p
\v 21 After all of this, Paul¦97865 decided in¦97867 his \nd spirit¦97869\nd* that¦97883 he would need to travel through¦97870 Macedonia¦97873 and¦97860 Achaia¦97876 enroute to Yerushalem¦97881, saying¦97882, “After I've been there¦97888 a while, I will also¦97874 need to go¦97878 to Rome¦97892.”
\v 22 He¦97910 sent¦97895 two¦97900 of¦97901 his workers, Timothy¦97907 and¦97896 Eratos, to¦97904 Macedonia¦97899 while he¦97910 stayed on¦97911 a¦97912 bit longer in¦97914 Asia¦97918 \add Minor\add*.
\rem /s1 The Riot in Ephesus
\p
\v 23 But¦97921 while he was still there, a major disturbance¦97926 hit the¦97923 Yeshua followers.
\v 24 A silversmith¦97937 named Demetrius¦97932 made¦97938 \add little\add* silver¦97941 shrines of¦97943 Artemis¦97943 and this business¦97952 brought¦97945 a tidy profit to¦97947 the¦97947 local craftsmen¦97948.
\v 25 Demetrius gathered all the¦97977 workers¦97963 together¦97955 and told them, “Men¦97969, you¦97971 all¦97971 know¦97971 that¦97974 our prosperity¦97978 comes from¦97973 this¦97974 business¦97976 of ours.
\v 26 But¦97982 you've seen yourselves and heard¦97986 from others that not only¦97990 Ephesus¦97993, but¦97982 almost¦97996 our entire¦97983 \add province of\add* Asia¦97999 \add Minor\add* has¦98005 become¦98019 persuaded¦98005 by¦98017 this¦98002 Paul¦98001 fellow, and now the¦98016 crowds¦98009 are saying¦98010 that what we make are not \add real\add* gods¦98015.
\v 27 However it's not¦98021 only¦98022 us¦98028 and our trade¦98030 that is coming¦98035 into¦98033 disrepute¦98034, but¦98023 the¦98038 temple¦98044 of the great goddess¦98041 Artemis is considered to¦98047 be¦98047 useless. This¦98024 could result in a loss of power for¦98045 the great Artemis that all¦98064 of Asia¦98066 \add Minor\add* and the¦98068 inhabited¦98069 world¦98069 have been worshipping¦98070.”
\p
\v 28 When¦98073 the gathering heard¦98072 this, they¦98083 became¦98076 very angry and¦98075 yelled out¦98083, “Artemis¦98087 of¦98088 the Ephesians is¦98085 great!”
\v 29 Then¦98089 the \add whole\add* city¦98094 was¦98090 filled¦98090 with¦98102 confusion¦98097 and the people rushed¦98099 to¦98103 the¦98104 theatre¦98105 and grabbed Gaius¦98108 and Aristarchus¦98110 from¦98111 Macedonia¦98111—travelling¦98113 companions¦98113 of¦98115 Paul's¦98115.
\v 30 Now Paul¦98117 wanted¦98122 to go there and speak to the crowd, but¦98119 the believers wouldn't let him¦98129 go.
\v 31 Also¦98134 some¦98133 of¦98136 the leaders¦98137 from Asia \add Minor\add* who were¦98145 friendly¦98141 towards him, sent¦98142 messengers to him to implore¦98145 him not¦98146 to go to the¦98151 theatre¦98152.
\v 32 Some of the people gathered there were yelling out¦98158 something, but¦98166 overall it was¦98159 just confusion, and¦98166 the majority of the people couldn't even tell you the reason why they¦98174 were there.
\v 33 Some people in the¦98177 crowd¦98178 selected Alexander¦98182 the Jew and¦98176 sent him¦98185 forward¦98183 to speak, so he motioned with his¦98193 hand¦98195 ready to explain the situation to the people.
\v 34 But¦98204 as soon as they recognised¦98202 that¦98205 he¦98207 was a Jew, people started yelling, “Artemis¦98222 of¦98223 the¦98223 Ephesians is¦98207 great¦98220,” and this continued for over¦98215 two¦98217 hours¦98216.
\p
\v 35 Eventually the¦98250 city¦98253 administrator was able to calm the¦98233 crowd¦98234 and tell¦98237 them, “Men¦98238, fellow Ephesians, what person¦98246 from around here doesn't know¦98249 that it's Ephesus that's¦98247 the temple¦98254 keeper¦98254 of the¦98258 great¦98259 Artemis¦98261 and of this image¦98264 that fell¦98264 down from Zeus?
\v 36 Since all¦98272 of this is¦98271 unquestionable¦98265, it¦98271 makes sense for everyone¦98272 to¦98274 calm down and¦98275 not do¦98278 anything reckless¦98277.
\v 37 These¦98283 men¦98282 that you¦98279 presented here haven't stolen anything from the¦98291 temple¦98287, nor¦98289 have they slandered¦98290 our¦98294 goddess¦98292.
\v 38 So if¦98296 Demetrius¦98299 and the silver craftsmen¦98307 with¦98305 him¦98306 have¦98308 a case against¦98309 anyone¦98311, they should¦98319 bring¦98315 it to the courts¦98314 and the proconsuls¦98317 and register the charges.
\v 39 But for the¦98322 rest of you, if¦98321 you have a grievance against others¦98322, it¦98337 needs to be¦98337 settled¦98337 in¦98331 a lawful¦98334 assembly¦98336
\v 40 because¦98339 we're in danger¦98340 of¦98343 being¦98350 charged with insurrection¦98343 about¦98344 what happened today¦98346. And¦98363 we would have¦98365 no defence that¦98352 we could¦98354 respond with concerning¦98358 this¦98362 disorderly¦98361 gathering¦98361.” Then¦98363 he¦98366 told them all to¦98356 go home.
\c 20
\s1 Paul travels to Macedonia and Achaia
\rem /s1 To Macedonia and Achaia; Through Macedonia and Greece; Paul Goes to Macedonia and Greece
\p
\v 1 After¦98369 all the¦98373 commotion¦98374 had died down, Paul¦98378 sent¦98375 for¦98375 the believers, and after¦98369 greeting¦98386 and encouraging them, he¦98388 left¦98388 \add Ephesus\add* to go¦98390 to Macedonia¦98393.\f + \fr 20:1 \ft As he'd already decided—see 19:21.\f*
\v 2 As he travelled through¦98394 the districts¦98398, he challenged the people there with¦98405 a lot of messages, until he eventually arrived in Greece¦98410.
\v 3 He'd been there about three¦98415 months¦98414 when he heard about a plot¦98420 by¦98422 some Jews¦98424 to waylay him¦98421 as he boarded a ship to return to Syria¦98431, so instead he decided to return through¦98442 Macedonia¦98444
\v 4 with¦98450 Sopater¦98456 son of Pyrrhus¦98457 from Berea, Aristarchus¦98461 and Secundus¦98463 from Thessalonica¦98459, Gaius¦98465 from Derbe, Tychicus¦98474 and Trophimus¦98476 from the¦98459 province of Asia¦98471 \add Minor\add*, and Timothy¦98469 all accompanying¦98445 him¦98450.
\v 5 They went ahead and¦98478 waited for us\f + \fr 20:5 \ft Luke (the writer) includes himself here.\f* at Troas¦98485.
\v 6 So¦98487 we¦98486 sailed¦98488 out from¦98494 Philippi¦98495 after the \it Flat Bread¦98493 Celebration\it*. We¦98486 arrived and meet the others there at¦98500 Troas¦98502 after a five-day trip, and then¦98487 stayed¦98513 on there for seven¦98515 days¦98505.
\s1 Paul's all-night discussions in Troas
\rem /s1 Paul's Last Visit to Troas; Paul's Final Visit to Troas; Paul's Farewell Visit to Troas; Eutychus Raised From the Dead at Troas
\p
\v 7 On¦98516 the Sunday we had¦98524 gathered¦98524 together to break¦98529 bread¦98530 together and¦98518 Paul¦98532 was discussing¦98533 many things with¦98534 them¦98534. As he was scheduled to leave the next¦98538 day¦98520, the meeting went¦98535 on¦98516 towards midnight¦98544
\v 8 and¦98546 there¦98545 were¦98545 many¦98549 lamps¦98548 in¦98550 the¦98551 upper¦98552 room¦98552 where¦98553 we¦98554 had¦98556 all¦98549 gathered¦98556.
\v 9 Now there was a young¦98561 man¦98561 named Eutychus¦98563 who was sitting¦98557 on the¦98565 window¦98567 sill, and as¦98574 Paul¦98576 went on, he fell¦98584 asleep and toppled down¦98588 from¦98585 the¦98586 third¦98587 storey. The¦98562 ones who reached him first thought he was dead¦98592,
\v 10 but¦98594 Paul¦98596 had come¦98593 downstairs and bent over Eutychus and held him¦98612 in¦98613 his¦98612 arms and said¦98606 to the¦98595 crowd, “Don't¦98607 worry, he's still alive.”
\v 11 Then¦98617 Paul went¦98633 back up¦98616 and broke¦98619 the¦98620 bread¦98621 with the others, eating a little and continuing to talk with them until¦98629 leaving at daybreak¦98630.
\v 12 The¦98639 locals took the¦98639 young man home and¦98643 were¦98644 very relieved that he was alive.
\s1 Sailing from Troas to Miletus
\rem /s1 From Troas to Miletus; The Voyage from Troas to Miletus; Paul Meets the Ephesian Elders; Paul's Farewell to the Ephesian Elders
\p
\v 13 We¦98647 went¦98662 ahead to the¦98654 ship¦98655 and departed for¦98667 Assos¦98660 where it had been¦98669 arranged that we'd pick up¦98663 Paul¦98665 because¦98667 he¦98672 wanted to walk there¦98661.
\v 14 So¦98678 when¦98677 we met him¦98688 at Assos¦98686, he¦98679 boarded the¦98685 ship and¦98678 we all sailed on to¦98690 Mitylene¦98691.
\v 15 After we left Mitylene, we were out from¦98692 Kios¦98698 the¦98699 next¦98702 day, in¦98705 Samos¦98706 the day after that, and¦98692 then¦98692 the¦98711 following¦98713 day we arrived¦98696 at Miletus¦98717.
\v 16 Paul¦98722 had¦98718 decided to sail¦98723 past¦98723 Ephesus¦98725 because¦98739 he was¦98738 hurrying¦98738 to try to get to Yerushalem¦98753 for¦98739 the¦98746 Day¦98747 of¦98749 Pentecost¦98749 so¦98726 he didn't¦98727 want to spend¦98732 time¦98732 in¦98751 Asia¦98737 \add Minor\add*.
\s1 Paul's farewell to the Ephesian elders
\rem /s1 Paul Speaks to the Ephesian Elders; Paul's Farewell Speech to the Elders of Ephesus
\p
\v 17 So¦98756 while we were at Miletus¦98758, he¦98763 sent¦98759 a message to Ephesus¦98761 for the¦98764 elders¦98765 of¦98766 the assembly¦98767 to come to him.
\v 18 When¦98769 they¦98770 arrived¦98770, he¦98777 told them¦98780, “You¦98781 all¦98781 know¦98782 that from¦98784 the very first¦98785 day¦98786 that I set¦98790 foot¦98790 in¦98790 Asia¦98793 \add Minor\add* and¦98769 for the entire¦98781 time¦98807 that I was there
\v 19 I humbly served¦98810 the \nd master¦98812\nd* even through tears¦98820 and¦98821 trials¦98822 when the¦98830 Jews¦98831 plotted to¦98826 destroy me¦98826.
\v 20 But¦98842 I didn't¦98839 shrink¦98834 back¦98834 from teaching you everything that¦98838 would help you, whether I was teaching in public¦98845 or in individual homes¦98848.
\v 21 I explained to both¦98853 Jews¦98852 and Greeks¦98855 how they needed to turn¦98860 their backs on their sins and towards¦98864 \nd God¦98859\nd* with faith¦98862 in our¦98870 \nd master¦98868 Yeshua¦98871\nd* the¦98866 \nd messiah¦98873\nd*.
\p
\v 22 However the¦98881 \nd spirit¦98882\nd* is now¦98876 forcing me towards Yerushalem¦98885 even though I don't¦98894 know¦98896 what will happen to¦98884 me there.
\v 23 I just know that recently in¦98904 every¦98904 city¦98906, the¦98901 holy¦98902 \nd spirit¦98900\nd* tells me that chains¦98913 and¦98915 hardships are¦98918 waiting for me.
\v 24 But¦98949 I've never regarded my own life as¦98939 being valuable—I only want to reach the end of¦98946 my life having served in the way that¦98954 the \nd master¦98960 Yeshua¦98961\nd* instructed me¦98946 to speak out the¦98966 good¦98967 message¦98967 about¦98923 \nd God's¦98971\nd* grace¦98969.
\p
\v 25 So¦98972 you see, I know¦98976 that¦98978 all¦98986 of¦98985 you from here where I passed¦98990 through¦98990 proclaiming¦98991 God's kingdom¦98993, you will never see me¦98985 again.
\v 26 That's¦99009 why I'm declaring to¦99002 all¦99002 of you¦99002 today¦99006 that¦99009 I'm not guilty of your judgement
\v 27 because¦99018 I didn't¦99017 avoid telling you¦99030 anything that¦99020 I had from \nd God¦99028\nd*.
\v 28 So now you elders look after your own lives and¦99035 those of your flock¦99038 because the¦99044 holy¦99045 \nd spirit¦99043\nd* chose you as overseers¦99048 to¦99049 shepherd¦99049 the¦99050 assembly¦99051 of God's¦99045 people who he¦99057 paid for with his¦99060 very own blood¦99062.
\v 29 I'm aware that¦99070 after¦99072 I leave, vicious wolves¦99077 will¦99071 come¦99071 in¦99071 amongst¦99079 you¦99080 and trying to steal the¦99083 flock¦99084.
\v 30 Even some of you¦99087 will rise¦99089 up¦99089 and¦99085 teach a message that's warped but¦99085 which will draw¦99095 some of the¦99093 believers away to¦99095 follow¦99097 it,
\v 31 so take great care. Remember¦99103 that¦99104 for¦99105 three¦99105 years¦99105, I¦99111 never stopped warning each¦99116 of you along with¦99112 my tears¦99113
\v 32 so now¦99120 I'm¦99121 leaving you¦99122 all¦99122 in \nd God's¦99127\nd* hands and¦99118 with the¦99129 message¦99130 of his¦99133 grace¦99132 which¦99134 is quite capable of helping you grow to¦99136 receive the¦99142 inheritance¦99143 that's¦99134 for all those who've been¦99148 purified.
\p
\v 33 I never envied anyone's gold¦99155 or silver¦99152 or \add fancy\add* clothes¦99157
\v 34 and you¦99163 yourselves¦99161 know¦99163 that¦99165 I laboured with my own hands¦99179 to¦99166 cover my expenses and for those¦99181 with me¦99176.
\v 35 Yes, I showed¦99185 you¦99186 all¦99186 that it's good to work and to take¦99191 care¦99191 of the weaker ones and to remember¦99195 the¦99197 message¦99198 of the \nd master¦99200 Yeshua¦99201\nd* when he¦99204 said¦99205 that it's better to be¦99211 a giver than¦99212 a taker.”
\p
\v 36 When¦99214 he'd finished speaking, Paul knelt¦99217 down and¦99214 prayed¦99224 with¦99221 them¦99223 all¦99222.
\v 37 Everyone¦99238 was sobbing as they¦99239 hugged and¦99232 kissed¦99239 him¦99240
\v 38 because what he'd said about¦99242 never seeing him again had¦99248 really distressed¦99241 them. Then¦99260 they¦99259 accompanied¦99259 him to¦99263 his¦99257 ship¦99265.
\c 21
\s1 Paul goes to Yerushalem
\rem /s1 On to Jerusalem; Paul Goes to Jerusalem; Paul's Journey to Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 When¦99268 it¦99269 was time for us¦99275 to sail, we¦99285 left them¦99282 and sailed directly across to Cos¦99288 before going on to Rhodes¦99295 the¦99294 next¦99292 day¦99289 and then¦99268 on to Patara¦99298.
\v 2 From there we found¦99302 a ship¦99303 heading to Phoenicia¦99306, so we boarded¦99307 that and¦99301 set off.
\v 3 After sighting Cyprus¦99313 and then¦99311 leaving it¦99317 behind on our left¦99318, we¦99319 sailed¦99319 on to Syria¦99321 and docked there¦99327 at¦99325 the port of Tyre¦99326 where the¦99330 ship¦99331 had cargo¦99335 to be unloaded¦99333.
\v 4 We¦99341 searched for the believers there¦99343 and¦99338 then¦99338 stayed on¦99341 with them for seven¦99345 days¦99344. They were¦99349 inspired by¦99350 the \nd spirit¦99352\nd* to tell¦99349 Paul¦99348 not¦99354 to go to Yerushalem¦99359.
\v 5 Once our time¦99367 was up there and everyone¦99372 knew that we¦99369 were¦99369 leaving, even the wives¦99374 and children¦99376 accompanied¦99370 us¦99363 out¦99364 of the¦99379 city¦99380 to¦99363 the port. We¦99369 all¦99372 knelt¦99382 on¦99386 the beach and prayed¦99390
\v 6 before we¦99393 continued on and boarded our ship¦99402 and they went¦99398 back to¦99406 their¦99407 own¦99408 homes¦99408.
\p
\v 7 So¦99410 we sailed from¦99414 Tyre¦99415 down to Ptolemais¦99419 where we were able to greet¦99421 the¦99422 brothers¦99423 and sisters¦99423 and stay overnight with¦99428 them¦99429.
\v 8 The¦99430 next¦99432 day¦99430 we continued on¦99430 to¦99440 Caesarea¦99441 and ended up in¦99443 Philip's¦99447 house¦99446. He was one of the \add original\add* seven¦99454 \add men who had been given the responsibility of distributing aid\add*—a preacher¦99449 of the¦99448 good¦99449 message¦99449, and we stayed with¦99456 him¦99457.
\v 9 Philip had four¦99464 unmarried daughters¦99462 still at home, and¦99459 they shared messages from God.
\v 10 Several days¦99471 later a¦99474 man named Agabus¦99480 came¦99473 from Yudea¦99477. He too shared messages from God
\v 11 and when he arrived, he picked¦99488 up¦99488 Paul's¦99492 belt and tied¦99493 it around his own feet¦99499 and hands¦99504, telling¦99508 them that the¦99511 holy¦99512 \nd spirit¦99510\nd* had told¦99508 him that this¦99519 is¦99508 how the owner of¦99515 the belt would be treated by the Jewish leaders in¦99523 Yerushalem¦99524 before they¦99528 would then hand¦99528 him over¦99528 to the non-Jews to be dealt with.
\p
\v 12 When¦99536 we heard¦99537 this described, both¦99542 we and¦99536 the¦99544 locals¦99545 begged Paul not¦99549 to go¦99551 to Yerushalem¦99554
\v 13 but he answered¦99556, “Oh come on, what's¦99566 all¦99567 this weeping¦99568 and¦99569 carrying on that's breaking¦99571 my¦99572 heart¦99574? Actually I'm¦99588 quite ready¦99587 not¦99577 only¦99578 to be tied¦99579 up, but even to be killed in¦99585 Yerushalem¦99586 as a testimony to the¦99592 \nd master¦99595 Yeshua¦99596\nd*.”
\p
\v 14 When¦99600 we saw that we couldn't change his mind, we didn't¦99598 say¦99604 anything else, figuring out that whatever the \nd master¦99608\nd* wills¦99610 for the situation would happen¦99614,
\rem /s1 Paul Arrives at Jerusalem
\v 15 and so after¦99616 these¦99621 several days¦99620, we¦99627 had¦99622 gotten everything ready and started off for Yerushalem¦99631.
\v 16 Some of the believers from Caesarea¦99639 accompanied us and¦99633 led us to the home of a man named Mnason¦99647 to stay—he was an older believer from Cyprus¦99649.
\s1 Paul visits Yacob and makes a vow
\rem /s1 Paul's Arrival at Jerusalem; Paul Visits James at Jerusalem
\p
\v 17 When¦99653 we arrived at¦99655 Yerushalem¦99656, the¦99661 brothers¦99662 and¦99653 sisters¦99662 there happily welcomed¦99658 us¦99660,
\v 18 and the next day¦99663, we accompanied Paul¦99672 to¦99675 go and see Yacob¦99676\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Yacob is commonly (but wrongly) called James in most older English Bibles.\f* and the¦99680 elders¦99681 of the assembly there.
\v 19 After greeting¦99685 them, Paul then¦99683 described all the things that \nd God¦99701\nd* had done¦99699 among the non-Jews as he¦99689 had served among them,
\v 20 and when they'd heard¦99711 all¦99735 that¦99732 they praised¦99712 \nd God¦99715\nd* and told Paul, “Brother¦99722, you've noticed how¦99723 many¦99723 thousands¦99724 of¦99724 Jews have believed¦99733 \add in the messiah\add* but¦99710 they still strive to¦99720 observe¦99721 \add Mosheh's\add* laws¦99739.
\v 21 But¦99742 they've heard reports that you've been teaching¦99747 the¦99750 Jews that live outside our country that they don't have to obey what Mosheh¦99749 \add wrote\add*—telling¦99758 them¦99762 that they don't have to have their¦99763 children¦99764 circumcised¦99761 and¦99742 that they don't have to follow our \add Jewish\add* customs¦99769.
\v 22 What¦99773 will happen \add now\add*, because \add the leaders here\add* will certainly¦99776 hear¦99781 that¦99783 you¦99784 have¦99784 arrived?
\v 23 So¦99786 here's what¦99788 we¦99790 suggest that¦99785 you¦99789 do¦99787: Four¦99794 of our men are¦99791 making a \add public\add* vow¦99795.
\v 24 Why don't you join these¦99800 four and join them¦99810 in the purification¦99802 ceremony \add at the temple\add* and pay their expenses in getting your heads¦99817 shaved¦99813. Then everyone¦99821 will¦99819 be¦99819 able to see that¦99824 it's false what they've¦99825 heard about¦99826 you, because you \add clearly\add* still comply with¦99803 \add Mosheh's\add* instructions¦99825.
\v 25 And¦99869 as for the¦99844 believers¦99845 who are non-Jews, we've informed them¦99867 of our recommendation that they should avoid anything that's been offered to¦99866 an idol¦99870, avoid consuming blood¦99873 and meat from¦99868 strangled¦99875 animals¦99875 and birds, and avoid sexual¦99877 immorality¦99877.”
\rem /s1 Paul Is Arrested
\p
\v 26 So the next day¦99887 Paul¦99880 took¦99881 the \add four\add* men¦99883 for¦99907 the purification¦99890 ceremony in the¦99894 temple¦99895 and there they announced¦99896 the date¦99887 when their vows would be fulfilled¦99898 and their sacrifices made.
\s1 Paul is captured inside the temple
\rem /s1 Paul Is Arrested in the Temple; Paul's work in Jerusalem
\p
\v 27 When¦99915 the¦99917 seven¦99919 days¦99921 \add of the vow\add* were¦99916 almost over, some Jews¦99930 from¦99927 \add the province of\add* Asia¦99929 \add Minor\add* noticed Paul in¦99934 the¦99935 temple¦99936. They¦99948 stirred up the¦99945 crowd¦99946 and¦99915 grabbed hold of him¦99953,
\v 28 yelling out¦99956, “Men¦99957 of Yisrael. Help¦99959 us here. This¦99976 is¦99961 the¦99962 man¦99963 who's¦99964 been teaching¦99981 all¦99978 around the world against Yisrael and against \add Mosheh's\add* law¦99970 and against this temple. What's more he brought¦99986 non-Jews here into¦99988 the temple and so has¦99993 defiled¦99993 this holy¦99995 place¦99996.”
\v 29 (\add They said this\add* because¦99999 they¦100011 had previously¦100001 seen¦100001 Trofimus¦100002 from¦100004 Ephesus in¦100005 the¦100006 city¦100007 with¦100008 Paul¦100019 and had assumed that¦100013 he had brought¦100017 him¦100009 into¦100014 the¦100015 temple¦100016.)
\p
\v 30 So¦100021 now the¦100022 whole¦100024 city¦100023 was¦100020 in an uproar, and the crowds rushed in, grabbed¦100031 Paul¦100033, and dragged¦100034 him¦100035 out of¦100033 the temple and hurriedly closed the temple gates.
\v 31 They were ready to¦100051 kill¦100051 Paul, but¦100048 the¦100054 commander¦100055 of¦100056 the Roman soldiers had heard that¦100058 all¦100059 Yerushalem¦100062 was in an¦100053 uproar.
\v 32 Taking some soldiers and officers, he immediately¦100064 ran¦100070 down¦100070 to the temple, and when the crowd saw¦100075 the¦100076 commander¦100077 and the soldiers they¦100082 stopped beating Paul¦100085.
\v 33 The¦100089 commander¦100090 came close and took¦100091 hold¦100091 of Paul and ordered¦100094 that he be¦100095 tied¦100095 up with¦100096 a pair of chains¦100096 while he tried to¦100095 find out who¦100100 he was¦100099 and what¦100104 he'd done¦100107 wrong.
\v 34 Various people in¦100115 the¦100116 crowd¦100117 were¦100113 shouting out¦100113 different things, and¦100122 not¦100119 being¦100121 able¦100121 to¦100124 make any¦100112 sense of it because¦100127 of all the¦100128 commotion¦100129, the commander¦100130 ordered¦100130 that Paul be¦100131 taken into¦100133 the¦100134 barracks¦100135.
\v 35 When¦100137 they got to the¦100141 stairs¦100142, the¦100149 soldiers¦100150 had to lift him¦100147 up above their heads because¦100151 of the frenzy of the¦100154 crowd¦100156,
\v 36 because¦100158 most of¦100161 the¦100161 people¦100162 were following¦100157 and shouting out¦100164, “Do away¦100166 with him¦100167!”
\s1 Paul's defence
\rem /s1 Paul Speaks to the Crowd; Paul Defends Himself
\p
\v 37 As they were able to carry him into¦100171 the fortress, Paul¦100175 called down to the commander¦100178, “Can I say¦100185 something¦100186 to you¦100188?”
\p “You¦100188 speak Greek!”, replied the¦100177 commander.
\v 38 “Aren't you¦100196 the Egyptian who¦100200 stirred up a group of¦100214 assassins¦100215 a while back and¦100206 led¦100207 them out¦100207 into¦100208 the¦100209 wilderness¦100210?”
\p
\v 39 “No, I'm¦100220 a Jew from¦100227 Tarsus¦100227 in Cilica—a citizen¦100235 of¦100230 an¦100233 important city¦100234,” said¦100216 Paul¦100219. “But¦100217 please, let me¦100241 speak¦100243 to¦100241 these people¦100246.”
\p
\v 40 The commander nodded, so the soldiers stood¦100256 Paul on¦100257 the¦100258 stairs¦100259 where he¦100277 gestured¦100262 to¦100265 the people¦100267. The crowd quietened down, and¦100249 so Paul spoke loudly in¦100278 his¦100248 Hebrew¦100280 language¦100281:
\c 22
\nb
\v 1 “Men, brothers¦100284, and¦100285 fathers¦100286, listen¦100287 to what I have to say for myself.”
\v 2 When¦100296 they heard¦100295 him speaking¦100308 Hebrew¦100299, they became even more¦100303 quiet¦100305 as he¦100308 continued,
\v 3 “I am¦100312 a¦100313 Jew who grew up¦100323 in¦100325 this¦100328 very city¦100327 to study under Gamaliel¦100332. I've been¦100333 raised according¦100335 to every part of our¦100337 ancestral¦100338 law¦100339, working hard to obey \nd God¦100343\nd* like all¦100345 of you¦100347 here today¦100350.
\v 4 I used to persecute¦100356 the followers of ‘The¦100354 Path’ even to death¦100359—tying up both¦100367 men¦100366 and women¦100369 and handing them over¦100362 to the prisons¦100364
\v 5 as¦100370 the¦100372 Chief¦100373 Priest¦100373 and all¦100381 the¦100382 council¦100383 of¦100383 elders¦100383 can tell \add you\add*. I took letters¦100387 from¦100384 them to our \add Jewish\add* brothers¦100393 in¦100409 Damascus¦100396 when I went¦100397 so that¦100409 I could¦100410 arrest the ones there¦100402 and bring¦100398 them back here¦100402 to Yerushalem¦100407 to be¦100410 punished¦100410.
\s1 Paul explains about his conversion
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Conversion
\r (Acts 9:1-19; 26:12-18)
\p
\v 6 “But¦100416 one time as I was travelling and¦100416 getting close to Damascus¦100419 by the middle of the day, suddenly¦100424 a bright light¦100432 from¦100426 the¦100427 sky¦100428 began to flash¦100429 around¦100429 me¦100435
\v 7 and¦100442 I fell¦100437 to¦100439 the¦100440 ground¦100441. I heard¦100443 a voice¦100444 asking me, ‘Saul, Saul, why¦100449 are¦100451 you¦100451 persecuting¦100451 me?’
\v 8 ‘Who¦100457 are you, master¦100459?’ I answered¦100454. And¦100462 the voice said¦100460, ‘I am¦100466 \nd Yeshua¦100468\nd*—the one from¦100470 \nd Nazareth¦100470\nd* that you are persecuting¦100473.’
\v 9 The¦100474 other¦100488 people with¦100476 me saw¦100482 the¦100479 light¦100481 but¦100475 didn't¦100490 hear¦100491 the¦100487 voice¦100489 that spoke¦100493 to¦100494 me.
\v 10 Then¦100496 I asked, ‘What¦100497 do I need to do, master?’ ‘Get up¦100506 and¦100496 continue into Damascus¦100509,’ the¦100500 master said¦100495. ‘When¦100496 you get there¦100510, you'll be¦100507 told everything that¦100515 you've been¦100517 assigned to do.’
\v 11 But¦100521 I¦100524 couldn't see anything because¦100526 the¦100527 brightness¦100528 of¦100531 the¦100529 light¦100530 \add had affected my eyes\add*, so my companions had to¦100539 lead¦100532 me¦100537 by¦100532 hand¦100532 into Damascus¦100540.
\p
\v 12 “In Damascus there was a man¦100544 named Ananias¦100541—a \add non-Jew\add* but¦100542 who was respected by¦100552 the¦100554 Jews¦100556 there because he strove to¦100547 obey \add Mosheh's\add* instructions about living¦100555.
\v 13 Ananias came¦100557 to where I was and standing¦100561 by¦100561 me¦100563 said¦100562, “Brother¦100565 Saul¦100564, receive¦100566 your sight¦100566!” That very hour¦100570 I was able to see again and look¦100572 at him¦100574
\v 14 and he¦100575 said¦100577, ‘The¦100580 \nd god¦100579\nd* of¦100582 our¦100582 ancestors¦100581 has decided in advance to reveal his¦100589 plans to you, and for you to see¦100591 the¦100596 sinless one—to hear¦100595 him¦100600 speaking
\v 15 because¦100601 he wants you to¦100605 inform people¦100607 everywhere what¦100609 you've¦100610 seen¦100610 and¦100611 heard¦100612.
\v 16 So¦100613 what else are¦100616 you waiting¦100616 for? Get up¦100617 and¦100613 wash¦100620 away¦100620 your¦100623 sins¦100622 by getting immersed¦100618 \add in water\add* as you request his¦100629 forgiveness.’
\s1 The crowd riots when Paul mentions preaching to non-Jews
\rem /s1 Paul Sent to the Gentiles; Paul's Call to Preach to the Gentiles
\p
\v 17 “Then¦100631 when I returned¦100633 to¦100634 Yerushalem¦100635 and¦100636 was praying¦100637 in¦100639 the¦100640 temple¦100641, I fell into a trance¦100645
\v 18 and¦100646 saw¦100647 \add @the¦100649 master¦100649\add* telling¦100650 me, ‘Get out¦100654 of¦100657 Yerushalem¦100658 in a hurry¦100652 because¦100659 they¦100661 won't¦100660 accept¦100661 what you're teaching¦100650 them about¦100665 me.’
\v 19 ‘\nd Master¦100669\nd*,’ I replied¦100668, ‘they¦100670 know¦100671 that¦100672 previously I was¦100674 the one imprisoning¦100675 and¦100676 beating¦100677 the people who believed¦100682 in¦100683 you¦100684,
\v 20 and when¦100686 Stephen¦100690 was killed¦100709 after teaching about¦100690 you¦100693, it was me standing¦100698 there and cheering on the¦100706 ones executing him¦100710 as I held their coats for them.’
\v 21 Then¦100711 \add @the master¦100712\add* told me¦100714, ‘Get going¦100715 now because¦100716 I'm sending¦100722 you¦100724 away¦100722 to¦100718 the non-Jews.’ ”
\rem /s1 Paul the Roman Citizen; Paul and the Roman Tribune
\p
\v 22 Until¦100729 this point in his message¦100732, the¦100731 crowd had been listening to Paul, but¦100727 now they¦100734 started shouting¦100738, “Do away¦100739 with this man¦100744! He doesn't deserve to live¦100750 in this world!”
\v 23 Then they yelled and threw some of their¦100758 clothes¦100759 down¦100757 onto the ground and threw dust¦100761 up into¦100763 the¦100764 air¦100766.
\rem /s1 Paul Reveals His Roman Citizenship
\v 24 So the¦100769 commander¦100770 ordered Paul to be brought¦100771 inside into¦100774 barracks¦100776 and ordered that he be flogged¦100778 and interrogated¦100779 to determine what he'd done that would make the crowd react like that.
\v 25 But¦100815 as¦100794 they¦100797 strapped¦100802 him down and¦100815 stretched¦100797 him out¦100797 for the flogging¦100820, Paul¦100809 asked the¦100805 centurion¦100807 standing¦100806 there, “It¦100818 is¦100818 legal for you¦100819 to¦100819 flog a Roman citizen who hasn't even been tried?”
\p
\v 26 When¦100823 he¦100835 heard¦100822 that¦100845, the¦100824 centurion¦100825 went¦100840 to the¦100832 commander¦100833 and¦100823 reported¦100835, “How are¦100840 we going¦100840 to handle this, because¦100843 this man¦100844 is¦100847 a Roman citizen?”
\p
\v 27 So¦100850 the¦100851 commander¦100852 went in to¦100855 where Paul was¦100865 and¦100850 asked¦100865 him¦100855, “Tell me¦100858 the truth. Are¦100862 you¦100860 a¦100861 Roman citizen?”
\p “Yes¦100867 I am,” said¦100854 Paul.
\p
\v 28 “Yeah, and¦100872 I paid a lot for mine as well,” mocked the¦100874 commander¦100875.
\p “Actually mine is through birth,” Paul¦100890 responded¦100891.
\p
\v 29 At this point, the soldiers who were about to interrogate¦100906 Paul left the room, and¦100910 even the commander¦100909 was¦100911 now afraid¦100911 after learning¦100912 that¦100913 he'd had a¦100914 Roman citizen tied¦100921 down to be¦100906 flogged.
\s1 Paul faces the Jewish council
\rem /s1 Paul's trials; Paul before the Council; Paul before the High Council; Before the Sanhedrin
\p
\v 30 The¦100922 next¦100924 day¦100922, the Roman commander¦100942 still wanted¦100925 to know¦100926 what was behind the anger of¦100924 the¦100934 Jews¦100935, so he had¦100954 Paul released¦100936 from the cell, but¦100923 brought¦100954 him¦100937 down¦100954 to a room where he'd ordered¦100942 the¦100945 chief¦100946 priests¦100946 and¦100947 the Jewish council¦100951 to convene. Paul was given a seat
\c 23
\nb
\v 1 and looking¦100960 directly at¦100964 the¦100964 council¦100965 members said¦100968, “Men¦100969, brothers¦100970, all¦100972 my life I have¦100975 lived¦100975 before \nd God¦100977\nd* as¦100975 a good¦100974 citizen¦100975 and with a clean conscience¦100973.”
\v 2 But¦100983 Ananias¦100985 the¦100982 chief¦100984 priest¦100984 commanded¦100987 one of¦100992 his¦100992 companions to¦100988 whack Paul on the¦100993 mouth¦100994.
\v 3 “\nd God¦101008\nd* will¦101013 strike¦101004 you,” said¦101003 Paul¦100997, “you grand show-off. You've been¦101010 brought here to¦101016 judge¦101014 me¦101015 as someone who knows the law and¦101011 yet you violate¦101020 the law by commanding¦101021 that I be¦101023 hit in the face.”
\p
\v 4 But¦101025 the¦101024 others standing¦101026 there said¦101027, “Are¦101032 you¦101032 insulting¦101032 \nd God's¦101031\nd* chief¦101029 priest¦101029?”
\p
\v 5 “Brothers¦101039,” said¦101033 Paul¦101036, “I didn't¦101037 realise that¦101040 he's¦101041 the chief¦101042 priest¦101042, because¦101044 the scriptures teach us not¦101037 to speak¦101051 wrongly¦101052 of¦101047 our leaders.”
\p
\v 6 But¦101082 then, knowing¦101053 that¦101057 the¦101070 council¦101071 consisted of some from the sect¦101062 of the Sadducees¦101062 and some from the Pharisees' party¦101079, Paul¦101056 continued loudly, “Men¦101072, brothers¦101073, like my father, I'm a member¦101075 of the Pharisees and I'm being¦101086 tried today concerning¦101080 the hope¦101081 and the resurrection¦101083 of the dead¦101084!”
\p
\v 7 Well, as soon as he said this¦101087, the council was¦101105 split as the¦101097 Pharisees¦101098 and¦101088 the Sadducees¦101102 started arguing.
\v 8 (Sadducees¦101108 say¦101111 that¦101123 the dead don't come back to¦101113 life, and there's no messengers¦101117 and no spirits¦101119, but¦101121 the Pharisees¦101120 believe there is.)
\v 9 So¦101126 then¦101126 there was a big kerfuffle because some¦101131 of the¦101133 teachers¦101135 of the law spoke up¦101130 for the¦101139 Pharisees¦101140 saying¦101144, “We¦101147 don't see anything wrong¦101146 with this¦101151 man¦101150. Maybe it was a spirit¦101154 or¦101157 one \add of God's\add* messengers¦101158 that¦101151 spoke to¦101156 him¦101156.”
\p
\v 10 But¦101162 the \add Roman\add* commander¦101172 was afraid¦101169 that they might tear¦101174 Paul apart¦101174 in the big argument, so he called for his soldiers to come¦101184 down¦101184 to uplift Paul and¦101162 return him¦101187 to the¦101195 barracks¦101196.
\p
\v 11 The¦101197 following¦101199 night¦101200, the¦101203 \nd master¦101204\nd* appeared to¦101202 Paul and told him¦101202, “Be¦101206 brave because¦101209 just like you testified¦101210 about me here in Yerushalem¦101216, so¦101217 too you'll tell them about me in Rome¦101222.”
\s1 The plan to kill Paul
\rem /s1 The Plan to Kill Paul; The Plot to Kill Paul; The Plot against Paul's Life
\p
\v 12 The¦101235 next day¦101227, some of¦101249 the¦101253 Jews¦101237 gathered¦101231 informally and¦101226 pledged not to¦101244 eat¦101244 or drink¦101246 until¦101247 they'd killed¦101251 Paul¦101255.
\v 13 There were¦101256 more¦101258 than¦101258 forty¦101259 of them in¦101263 this¦101263 conspiracy¦101265,
\v 14 and they approached¦101269 the¦101270 chief¦101271 priests¦101271 and elders¦101274 and told them, “We've¦101276 made an oath to¦101280 taste¦101280 nothing¦101279 until¦101283 we've killed¦101286 Paul¦101288, otherwise we'll bring a curse¦101276 on ourselves¦101278.
\v 15 So¦101290 you guys assemble the¦101304 council¦101305 and report¦101300 to the¦101301 commander¦101302 so¦101306 that¦101332 he'll bring¦101309 him¦101310 down¦101309 to you¦101292 all¦101292 as¦101315 if you need more¦101318 information about¦101321 his actions, and then we'll have an opportunity to kill¦101333 before¦101325 he gets here.
\p
\v 16 But¦101351 a nephew of¦101343 Paul had heard¦101339 about¦101343 this ambush¦101348 and¦101351 went in¦101352 to the¦101354 barracks¦101356 to inform Paul.
\v 17 Paul¦101363 then¦101361 called¦101360 one¦101364 of¦101365 the Roman centurions¦101366 and¦101361 told¦101368 him to take the¦101369 young¦101370 man¦101370 to the¦101375 commander¦101376 because¦101378 he¦101377 had something¦101381 to tell¦101368 him.
\v 18 So¦101385 the centurion took¦101386 him¦101387 to the¦101390 commander¦101391, explaining, “The¦101394 prisoner¦101395 Paul¦101396, called¦101397 me and¦101392 asked¦101399 me to bring¦101388 this¦101400 young¦101402 man¦101402 to you because he¦101393 has something¦101408 to report to you.”
\p
\v 19 The¦101417 commander¦101418 took¦101411 the young man by¦101422 the¦101414 hand¦101415 and led him¦101416 into his¦101416 private office and asked, “What¦101425 do you¦101428 have to¦101429 tell me¦101430?”
\p
\v 20 “The¦101434 Jews¦101435 have decided¦101436 to ask¦101438 you,” he¦101431 said¦101431, “that¦101437 tomorrow¦101441 you bring¦101444 Paul¦101443 down¦101444 to the¦101446 council¦101447 as¦101451 if they wanted to question him¦101460 further.
\v 21 But¦101485 don't¦101463 listen to them¦101465 because¦101467 over forty¦101473 of¦101469 their¦101469 men¦101471 will¦101466 be¦101464 lying¦101466 in¦101466 wait¦101466 for him, and¦101485 they've sworn not¦101463 to eat¦101478 or drink¦101480 until¦101481 they've killed¦101483 him. In¦101466 fact, they're¦101488 all set up already, just waiting¦101490 for your consent.”
\p
\v 22 The¦101495 commander¦101498 told¦101503 the¦101500 young¦101501 man¦101501 not to¦101505 tell¦101505 anyone¦101504 else about the ambush and dismissed him.
\s1 Paul is escorted away in the night
\rem /s1 Paul Is Sent to Caesarea; Paul Transferred to Caesarea; Paul Is Sent to Governor Felix; Paul Sent to Felix the Governor
\p
\v 23 Then¦101511 the¦101516 commander called¦101512 in two of¦101516 his centurions¦101517 and ordered them to prepare¦101519 two-hundred soldiers to travel as far¦101524 as Caesarea¦101525, supported by seventy¦101528 mounted soldiers and two-hundred more with spears¦101530. They were to be¦101523 ready to leave by 9pm,
\v 24 along with a horse for Paul¦101543 to ride, and¦101538 were to get him safely¦101544 to Governor¦101548 Felix¦101546.
\v 25 Then the¦101571 commander wrote¦101565 a letter¦101568 ready to take:
\p
\v 26 “Greetings¦101584 to¦101584 the¦101580 most¦101581 excellent¦101581 Governor¦101582 Felix¦101583 from Claudius Lysias¦101578.
\v 27 I'm writing to you about this¦101587 man¦101586 who had been¦101591 captured¦101591 by¦101592 the¦101593 Jews¦101594 here and¦101595 they were about to kill¦101597 him when I moved in with¦101601 my soldiers because he¦101615 is¦101615 a Roman citizen.
\v 28 I wanted¦101616 to know¦101619 what they¦101626 were¦101626 accusing him of¦101633, so I took him down¦101628 to their¦101633 council¦101632 meeting,
\v 29 but¦101645 I discovered that it was just some detail about¦101639 their¦101643 \add Jewish\add* law¦101642 and nothing¦101644 that should result in death¦101647 or¦101648 imprisonment.
\v 30 Then¦101654 when I learnt that they have a plot¦101656 to try to ambush and kill him, I sent¦101668 him immediately¦101665 to you, and I'll order his accusers¦101674 to bring their case before¦101680 you.”
\p
\v 31 So¦101685 the¦101683 soldiers¦101686 carried out their instructions and took¦101691 Paul¦101693 as far as Antipatris¦101700 during the night¦101697.
\v 32 The¦101701 next¦101703 morning the foot soldiers returned¦101712 to the¦101714 barracks¦101715, leaving the mounted soldiers to take him¦101710 on¦101701
\v 33 to Caesarea¦101720, where they¦101727 presented¦101727 Paul¦101730 to the¦101725 governor¦101726 along with the¦101723 letter¦101724.
\v 34 After reading¦101732 the letter the governor asked¦101737 Paul what¦101740 province¦101741 that he's¦101742 from and¦101733 found out that he was from Cilicia¦101747.
\v 35 Then he ordered¦101763 Paul to¦101772 be¦101772 kept under guard¦101772 \add in the prison\add* in Herod's¦101771 palace until he would hear¦101752 from¦101753 him¦101773 in the future once his accusers¦101758 got there.
\c 24
\s1 The Jews accuse Paul before Governor Felix
\rem /s1 The Case against Paul; Paul before Felix at Caesarea; Paul Appears before Felix; The Trial Before Felix
\p
\v 1 Five¦101777 days¦101778 later¦101774, Ananias¦101782 the¦101780 chief¦101781 priest¦101781 arrived along with¦101783 some¦101786 elders¦101785 and¦101787 an¦101788 attorney¦101788 named Tertullos¦101789, to¦101793 bring the charges against¦101795 Paul¦101797 before Governor¦101794 \add Felix\add*.
\v 2 Once he was called¦101798 to¦101802 speak, Tertullos¦101804 began¦101801 his¦101800 accusation¦101802 of¦101812 Paul:
\p “We have seen peace¦101807 under your rule and¦101799 the¦101803 helpful reforms¦101812 that¦101817 this¦101817 nation¦101816 has experienced through¦101818 your provision¦101821,
\v 3 most¦101827 excellent¦101827 Felix¦101828, and we¦101826 gratefully welcome¦101826 everything¦101822 you've done and everywhere¦101825 you've done it¦101826.
\v 4 But¦101833 in order¦101832 not¦101834 to oblige you longer than necessary, in your¦101846 gentleness¦101847 we request you to listen¦101841 to our brief¦101844 statement.
\v 5 This¦101852 man¦101851 here has¦101848 been found¦101848 to¦101859 be a troublemaker, stirring¦101856 up riots among the¦101860 Jews¦101861 throughout the¦101864 inhabited¦101865 world¦101865, and¦101867 as a ring-leader of¦101868 the¦101869 Nazarite¦101870 sect¦101871
\v 6 he even¦101873 attempted¦101876 to profane¦101877 the¦101874 temple¦101875 where we¦101880 were able to arrest him.
\v 7 ◘
\v 8 When you examine¦101912 him yourself¦101911 you'll¦101910 discover all¦101914 these¦101915 things that¦101917 we're¦101918 accusing¦101919 him of¦101917.”
\v 9 The¦101925 Jews¦101926 who had¦101930 come also¦101924 affirmed all the¦101925 accusations.
\s1 Paul's defence against the accusations
\rem /s1 Paul's Defense before Felix
\p
\v 10 So¦101932 the¦101938 governor¦101939 nodded¦101936 for¦101941 Paul¦101935 to¦101948 speak¦101940 and¦101932 he started,
\p “I happily defend¦101956 myself¦101955 today knowing¦101950 that¦101949 you¦101945 have¦101936 many¦101942 years¦101943 of experience ruling \add in this region\add*.
\v 11 You¦101958 can quickly establish that it was less than twelve¦101968 days¦101966 ago that I¦101971 arrived in¦101973 Yerushalem¦101975 to¦101959 worship there¦101964.
\v 12 They¦101982 never found¦101982 me¦101983 in the¦101980 temple¦101981 arguing with¦101984 anyone¦101985 or stirring up the crowd¦101991, and¦101977 nor¦101996 was I doing that in the \add ≈Jewish¦101995 meeting¦101995 halls¦101995\add* or anywhere in the¦101998 city¦101999
\v 13 and so they¦102004 can't provide evidence to you¦102005 to support any of their accusations¦102011.
\v 14 However, I must confess¦102013 that I follow ‘The¦102019 Path’ that they¦102022 call¦102022 a sect¦102023 which¦102021 serves¦102025 the \nd God¦102028\nd* of our ancestors¦102027 and¦102035 believes¦102029 everything in¦102030 \add Mosheh's\add* law¦102034 as well as the writings of the¦102038 prophets¦102039.
\v 15 We put our trust in¦102044 the¦102045 same God that¦102047 they themselves¦102049 follow, and we all anticipate a resurrection¦102052 of¦102056 those who serve God and those who don't.
\v 16 Because of this¦102061, I also¦102062 strive to¦102069 maintain a clean conscience¦102067 in¦102060 everything¦102077, before \nd God¦102072\nd* and¦102073 the¦102074 people¦102075.
\p
\v 17 After several years¦102079 away, I arrived¦102089 \add in Yerushalem\add* bringing offerings¦102091 to¦102084 help my¦102088 own people and¦102090 before God.
\v 18 That¦102093 was when they¦102095 found¦102095 me in¦102098 the¦102099 temple¦102100 after going through the purification¦102097 ceremony, and without either a crowd¦102103 or a commotion¦102106 around me.
\v 19 But¦102120 there were some¦102107 Jews¦102113 from¦102110 \add the province of\add* Asia¦102112 \add Minor\add* who are the ones who should be appearing here before¦102117 you¦102118, and¦102120 they should be the ones accusing¦102121 me if¦102122 they could find anything¦102123 against¦102125 me.
\v 20 Even these¦102130 ones here now should¦102131 explain what¦102133 their council¦102142 found¦102134 me¦102139 guilty of¦102139,
\v 21 other than¦102143 my calling out¦102149 that¦102148 I was being¦102160 judged¦102160 because I believe in the resurrection¦102157 of¦102158 the dead¦102158.
\p
\v 22 But¦102167 Felix¦102171 who was already familiar with followers of ‘The¦102170 Path’, announced that the¦102176 trial would be¦102186 adjourned until Commander¦102184 Lysias¦102182 arrived when he¦102165 would investigate¦102186 further.
\v 23 He ordered the¦102192 centurion¦102194 to keep¦102195 Paul confined, but¦102200 under relaxed¦102201 conditions and¦102200 allowing any of¦102205 his¦102207 friends to bring him¦102211 anything he needed.
\s1 Paul speaks to the governor and his wife
\rem /s1 Paul Held in Custody; Paul before Felix and Drusilla
\p
\v 24 However, several days¦102214 later¦102212 Felix¦102219 returned there, \add this time\add* with¦102220 his¦102222 wife¦102224 Drusilla¦102221 who was Jewish. He¦102229 sent¦102229 for¦102229 Paul¦102231 and¦102232 listened to him¦102234 explain about¦102235 faith¦102241 in¦102237 the¦102218 \nd messiah¦102238\nd*.
\v 25 But¦102248 when the subject turned to righteousness¦102246 and self-control¦102249 and the coming¦102255 judgement¦102253, Felix¦102262 became¦102260 very uncomfortable and told him¦102244, “That's¦102254 enough for now. If I have time¦102268 in the future, I'll¦102272 send for you¦102273.”
\v 26 However he was¦102294 also¦102276 hoping¦102277 that¦102278 Paul¦102284 would give¦102280 him money¦102279, so he often sent¦102292 for¦102292 him and spoke with¦102295 him.
\p
\v 27 This went on for two¦102296 years¦102296, until Felix was replaced as governor by Porcius¦102303 Festus¦102304, but¦102297 wanting¦102305 to¦102309 stay in the good books of the¦102310 Jews¦102311, Felix left¦102314 Paul¦102317 confined.
\c 25
\s1 Tried yet again, Paul appeals to Caesar
\rem /s1 The Trial Before Festus; Paul Appeals to the Emperor; Paul Appears before Festus
\p
\v 1 After¦102325 Festus¦102319 had¦102321 been in¦102321 the area for three¦102326 days¦102327, he made the uphill trip from¦102331 Caesarea¦102332 to¦102329 Yerushalem¦102330.
\v 2 There the¦102338 chief¦102340 priests¦102340 and the Jewish leaders¦102343 repeated their case against¦102346 Paul¦102348, and they¦102350 implored¦102350 Festus,
\v 3 asking as a special favour¦102353, that he¦102358 would order Paul to be sent¦102358 to Yerushalem¦102362 (because they wanted to ambush¦102363 and kill¦102365 him¦102366 on¦102367 the¦102368 way¦102369).
\v 4 But¦102383 Festus¦102373 replied that¦102370 he'd keep¦102375 Paul¦102377 in¦102378 Caesarea¦102380 and¦102383 he was heading back there¦102372 soon,
\v 5 so he¦102394 told¦102394 them¦102407, “Some of your leaders can go¦102397 there with¦102397 me, and if¦102398 the¦102403 man¦102404 has¦102397 done anything¦102399 wrong, they can explain their accusations¦102407 there.”
\p
\v 6 After staying¦102409 seven or¦102418 eight¦102417 days¦102413 in Yerushalem, Festus and company travelled back to¦102421 Caesarea¦102422, and the next¦102425 day¦102424 he¦102430 sat¦102426 at the judge's bench and ordered¦102430 that Paul¦102432 be¦102434 brought¦102434 in.
\v 7 When¦102436 he arrived¦102435, the¦102440 Jews¦102444 who'd come¦102443 from¦102441 Yerushalem¦102442 stood¦102438 facing Paul and¦102446 presented many¦102445 heavy¦102447 charges¦102448 which¦102454 they¦102456 weren't¦102456 able¦102456 to¦102458 prove.
\v 8 Paul¦102460 defended¦102461 himself, stating that¦102463 he hadn't sinned¦102478 against any Jewish law or against the¦102472 temple¦102473, nor¦102474 against any Roman law.
\p
\v 9 However, Festus¦102480 wanted¦102483 to be able to grant¦102488 a favour¦102487 to the¦102490 Jews¦102485, so in response to Paul¦102491 he¦102492 asked, “Are¦102493 you willing¦102493 to be returned to Yerushalem¦102495 so that I can judge¦102501 you about¦102498 these¦102499 things there¦102497?”
\p
\v 10 “I'm¦102514 here facing Caesar's¦102512 representative,” Paul¦102507 replied¦102504. “and it's¦102517 quite appropriate¦102517 for me¦102516 to be¦102518 judged¦102518 here. I've¦102522 done¦102522 nothing¦102520 wrong¦102522 to these Jews¦102519 as¦102523 you¦102525 can no doubt see for yourself.
\v 11 If¦102529 I had¦102537 done¦102537 anything wrong¦102533, anything worthy¦102535 of the death sentence, then I'm¦102533 ready to accept death. On¦102544 the other¦102544 hand, if¦102543 none of their accusations¦102549 are¦102549 true¦102546, no¦102551 one¦102551 can hand me¦102552 over to them¦102555. I appeal¦102558 to Caesar¦102557.”
\p
\v 12 So Festus¦102561 conferred with¦102562 his legal counsel¦102566 and answered¦102567, “Well, you've appealed¦102569 to Caesar so you'll¦102572 go¦102572 to Caesar!”
\s1 King Agrippa wants to hear from Paul
\rem /s1 Festus Consults King Agrippa; Paul before Agrippa and Bernice
\p
\v 13 Several days¦102573 later, King¦102579 Agrippa¦102577 and¦102580 \add his sister\add* Bernice¦102581 arrived¦102582 in Caesarea¦102585 to¦102584 meet Festus¦102589.
\v 14 As¦102590 they¦102594 \add had planned to\add* stay¦102594 on for a few days¦102593, Festus¦102598 mentioned about¦102603 Paul¦102605 to the¦102599 king¦102600, telling¦102606 him, “There's¦102609 a prisoner¦102613 here¦102596 that Felix¦102612 left¦102610 behind.
\v 15 When I was in¦102618 Yerushalem¦102619, the¦102622 chief¦102623 priests¦102623 and¦102624 Jewish elders¦102626 reported¦102620 to me¦102617, wanting a conviction¦102632 of¦102627 this man.
\v 16 I told them that¦102637 it's¦102639 not¦102638 the Roman way to convict someone until they've been able to face¦102654 their accusers¦102657 and¦102660 make their defence¦102661 against the¦102656 accusations¦102657.
\v 17 So¦102667 as soon as they arrived here¦102669, on the very next¦102676 day¦102675 I sat¦102677 on the judge's bench and ordered¦102681 the¦102683 man¦102684 to¦102682 be¦102682 brought¦102682 in
\v 18 that they were¦102692 accusing¦102689, but they didn't¦102690 raise even one¦102690 of¦102694 the¦102688 charges¦102691 that I expected.
\v 19 Instead they¦102708 raised some issues about their¦102705 own¦102706 beliefs and¦102712 about some dead person called \nd Yeshua¦102715\nd* that Paul¦102720 reckoned was¦102718 still living¦102721.
\v 20 I was¦102731 puzzled about this debate¦102730 and¦102723 asked¦102731 him if¦102732 he wanted¦102733 to¦102735 be¦102735 judged¦102740 about these¦102742 things in Yerushalem¦102738.
\v 21 But¦102744 Paul¦102745 appealed¦102746 to be kept safe from them until he could¦102760 face the¦102751 emperor, so I ordered¦102755 him to be kept \add in prison\add* until I can send¦102760 him to Rome.”
\p
\v 22 “I'd quite like to¦102767 hear¦102776 him¦102782 myself¦102773,” Agrippa¦102765 said¦102780.
\rem /s1 Paul Before Agrippa; Paul Speaks to Agrippa; Paul Brought before Agrippa
\p “Well, tomorrow¦102779,” replied¦102765 Festus¦102769, “you'll¦102781 be¦102781 able to hear from¦102782 him.”
\p
\v 23 So¦102784 the next¦102785 day¦102783, \add King\add* Agrippa¦102788 and Bernice¦102791 formally arrived with¦102792 all their attendants and entered the¦102798 auditorium¦102800 to greet the commanders¦102804 and the prominent¦102809 men¦102806 of¦102785 the¦102811 city¦102812, and then Festus¦102816 ordered¦102814 for Paul¦102819 to be brought¦102817 in¦102796.
\v 24 Festus¦102823 started, “King¦102825 Agrippa¦102824 and everyone¦102827 present¦102829, observe¦102832 the¦102838 prisoner who a multitude¦102839 of¦102840 Jews¦102841 pleaded¦102842 with¦102844 me¦102844 about¦102834 both¦102846 in¦102845 Yerushalem¦102847 and here¦102849, that¦102833 he doesn't deserve to¦102853 still be¦102853 alive.
\v 25 But¦102870 I haven't discovered anything that he's done¦102867 that's worthy¦102863 of¦102865 a death¦102865 sentence, and¦102870 since he himself¦102869 appealed¦102874 to¦102875 the¦102875 emperor¦102876, I judged¦102877 that that's where he should be¦102878 sent¦102878.
\v 26 The¦102903 problem is that I don't¦102888 have any¦102884 charge to write to my¦102886 master¦102887 and¦102896 so I've brought¦102892 the¦102903 prisoner out in front¦102894 of¦102897 you so that after you examine¦102904 him¦102893, I might have something¦102908 I can write,
\v 27 because¦102912 it¦102914 doesn't seem logical to me¦102913 to transport a prisoner¦102916 \add to Rome\add* if there's no charges¦102922 specified against¦102920 him¦102921.”
\c 26
\s1 Paul's defence before Festus and Agrippa
\rem /s1 Paul Defends Himself before Agrippa
\p
\v 1 So then Agrippa¦102924 told¦102929 Paul¦102928, “Okay, you¦102931 can give your side now then.”
\m So Paul gestured with his¦102941 hand¦102942 and¦102925 began:
\p
\v 2 “Concerning¦102944 all¦102945 the charges which¦102946 have¦102952 been brought against me by¦102948 the Jews¦102949, King¦102950 Agrippa¦102951, I consider¦102952 myself¦102953 fortunate
\v 3 because you're an expert on Jewish customs¦102976 and¦102978 issues, so I¦102982 ask you to listen¦102985 to me¦102986 patiently¦102984.
\p
\v 4 “All¦103007 the¦102987 Jews know¦103006 about¦102991 my early life, from¦102993 my upbringing in my own country¦103001 and¦103004 then in Yerushalem¦103005.
\v 5 They¦103014 also know¦103010, and could¦103014 even testify¦103015 to¦103015 you if¦103013 they¦103014 would admit it, that¦103016 I followed our¦103022 religious¦103023 laws very closely as a member¦103025 of¦103022 the¦103018 Pharisees¦103025 party¦103025.
\v 6 Yet now¦103027 I stand¦103041 judged¦103042 for¦103028 believing in the¦103029 same hope as our ancestors¦103034—that hope promised¦103036 by¦103038 our \nd God¦103040\nd*
\v 7 who we twelve¦103046 tribes¦103046 diligently serve¦103053 day¦103052 and¦103051 night¦103050 in¦103048 order to¦103056 obtain—the hope¦103060, \add ≈your majesty\add*, for which¦103044 I'm¦103061 being¦103061 indicted¦103061.
\v 8 So why¦103068 do you¦103072 all¦103072 find it¦103070 unbelievable¦103069 that \nd God¦103075\nd* can raise¦103077 the¦103074 dead¦103076?
\p
\v 9 “I too \add originally\add* thought it¦103090 necessary¦103090 to do¦103093 many¦103091 things to oppose the¦103084 cause of¦103089 this \nd Yeshua¦103087\nd* from¦103089 \nd Nazareth¦103089\nd*.
\v 10 So in Yerushalem¦103100 I worked to have¦103115 many¦103102 of these innocent believers locked¦103109 up¦103109 in prison¦103108 with the¦103110 authority¦103114 of the¦103112 chief¦103113 priests¦103113, or if they were to be killed¦103116, I would add my vote¦103121 against¦103119 them¦103118.
\v 11 And¦103122 in¦103123 all¦103124 the¦103125 \add ≈Jewish¦103126 meeting¦103126 halls¦103126\add*, \add when I found believers\add* I punished¦103128 them and forced¦103130 them to curse \add God\add* and treated them with extreme anger, even¦103138 travelling to further away towns.
\s1 Paul tells about his commission
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Conversion
\rem /r (Acts 9:1-19; 22:6-16)
\p
\v 12 “So it was one time travelling to¦103147 Damascus¦103149 carrying permission¦103153 letters from the¦103148 chief¦103157 priests¦103157
\v 13 that in the middle¦103159 of¦103169 the day¦103158, \add ≈your majesty\add*, I saw¦103163 a bright¦103168 light¦103173 from¦103165 the sky¦103165—even brighter than the¦103169 sun¦103170—and it shone¦103171 on¦103160 me¦103177 and my companions.
\v 14 All¦103179 of¦103183 us¦103183 fell¦103182 down¦103182 to the¦103185 ground¦103186 and¦103180 I heard¦103187 a voice¦103188 speaking¦103190 to me in¦103195 Hebrew¦103196 saying¦103190, ‘Saul, Saul, why¦103200 are¦103202 you persecuting¦103202 me? Life will¦103202 be¦103207 difficult for¦103204 you if you resist me.’
\p
\v 15 ‘Who¦103211 are¦103212 you¦103212, master¦103213?’, I¦103218 asked.
\p “And¦103215 the master responded¦103210, ‘I am¦103219 \nd Yeshua¦103220\nd*, the one you're¦103212 persecuting¦103223.
\v 16 Now get up¦103225, because¦103234 I¦103235 let you see me¦103246 in¦103232 order to appoint¦103237 you as my servant and my witness¦103242—to tell the¦103229 people what you've seen¦103249 and what you will¦103249 be¦103249 shown.
\v 17 I will rescue¦103251 you from your own people¦103255 and¦103256 from the¦103258 non-Jews that I'm¦103262 sending¦103265 you out¦103265 to¦103260,
\v 18 to open¦103268 their minds and for them¦103270 to turn¦103274 back¦103274 from darkness¦103276 to light¦103278 and from Satan's¦103284 power¦103282 to \nd God¦103287\nd*, so that¦103299 they can be forgiven¦103291 for their sins¦103292 and receive¦103289 an inheritance among¦103295 the¦103296 ones who've been¦103297 made guiltless by¦103298 their faith¦103298 in¦103300 me¦103301.’
\s1 Paul tells about his ministry
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Work; Paul Tells of His Preaching
\p
\v 19 “So then, King¦103303 Agrippa¦103304, I¦103306 couldn't disobey that vision¦103310
\v 20 so I preached first¦103315 to those in Damascus¦103314 and then in Yerushalem¦103319 and all¦103321 of Yudea¦103327, and then in the towns of the non-Jews. I preached about¦103327 turning from their sins and turning back¦103336 to \nd God¦103339\nd*, and then actually living like people who had repented¦103334.
\v 21 It was because of these¦103346 things that the Jews¦103349 grabbed me in the¦103355 temple¦103356 and tried to¦103358 do me in.
\v 22 So¦103360 it is that with \nd God's¦103366\nd* help¦103359 until¦103367 this¦103370 very day¦103369 I have¦103371 stood¦103371 testifying¦103372 to¦103374 commoners and leaders, teaching¦103380 nothing¦103378 other than what¦103381 Mosheh¦103389 and the¦103383 prophets¦103384 had already written that would happen¦103387—
\v 23 that the \nd messiah¦103393\nd* would \add come and\add* suffer¦103391 and as the first¦103395 of the dead¦103398 to come back to life, he is¦103400 going¦103400 to be¦103402 proclaiming¦103402 light¦103399 to both¦103404 Jews and non-Jews.”
\rem /s1 Paul Appeals to Agrippa to Believe
\p
\v 24 Then¦103410 speaking¦103419 about¦103411 Paul's¦103421 defence¦103412, Governor Festus¦103414 said¦103419, “Paul, you're stark, raving¦103420 mad¦103420! All your education has turned¦103429 you into an idiot.”
\p
\v 25 But¦103440 Paul¦103432 said¦103435, “I haven't gone mad¦103434, most¦103436 excellent¦103436 Festus¦103437, but¦103438 I'm¦103434 speaking¦103443 the¦103430 truth¦103439 in all seriousness.
\v 26 The¦103448 king¦103449 knows what I'm talking¦103454 about¦103446 and that's why I'm speaking¦103454 boldly¦103453, because¦103445 none of these¦103459 things were done¦103468 in¦103466 a corner¦103467 and I'm sure that none of it has¦103468 escaped¦103455 his notice¦103455.
\v 27 King¦103471 Agrippa¦103473, do you believe¦103470 what the¦103474 prophets¦103475 \add wrote\add*? Yes, I'm sure you do.”
\p
\v 28 But¦103480 Agrippa¦103481 answered¦103481 Paul¦103484, “Do you think you can talk me¦103488 into becoming a follower¦103491 of¦103491 the¦103491 messiah¦103491 in¦103486 just that short time¦103487?”
\p
\v 29 “I hope¦103498 to¦103520 \nd God¦103502\nd*”, said¦103496 Paul¦103496, “whether it takes a short time or a long time, that both¦103503 you¦103512 and¦103495 the¦103516 others listening today¦103519 will become¦103520 just like me¦103518 except¦103527 for these¦103530 chains¦103529.”
\p
\v 30 Then the¦103537 king¦103538 and the¦103540 governor¦103541 both¦103543 stood¦103535, along with Bernice¦103544 and the others sitting¦103547 with them¦103548
\v 31 and¦103549 as they left they were¦103551 saying¦103554 to¦103552 each other, “That¦103555 man¦103566 hasn't done¦103564 anything deserving of¦103558 death¦103558 or¦103560 imprisonment.”
\p
\v 32 “Yes, he could¦103574 have¦103573 been set free,” replied Agrippa¦103568, “if he hadn't appealed¦103580 to¦103571 Caesar¦103581.”
\c 27
\s1 Paul is escorted off overseas
\rem /s1 Paul Sails for Rome; Paul's journey to Rome
\p
\v 1 So¦103583 when¦103582 it¦103584 was¦103584 considered the right season to sail¦103586 to Italy¦103590, Paul¦103595 and¦103596 some¦103597 other¦103598 prisoners¦103599 were¦103591 put under the guard of¦103604 a centurion¦103600 named Julius (from the Augustan¦103605 Regiment).
\v 2 We¦103619 all boarded¦103606 on¦103608 a ship¦103608 from Adramyttium¦103609 that was about¦103623 to sail¦103612 to various places¦103618 along¦103615 the¦103614 coast of¦103623 Asia¦103617 \add Minor\add*. Aristarchus¦103623, a Macedonian¦103625 man from Thessalonica¦103626, was with¦103621 us¦103622 as we¦103619 departed.
\p
\v 3 The¦103627 next¦103629 day¦103629 we¦103630 docked at¦103631 Tsidon¦103632 where Julius¦103636 very kindly allowed Paul¦103638 to¦103638 visit some of¦103646 his friends¦103643 so they could provide for his needs.
\v 4 When¦103648 we¦103650 left again, the ship stayed between Cyprus¦103652 and¦103648 the mainland because¦103653 of¦103653 the unfavourable winds¦103656,
\v 5 then¦103660 after sailing¦103668 past Cilicia¦103665 and¦103666 Pamfylia¦103667, we¦103669 docked at Myra¦103673 in \add the province\add* of¦103675 Lycia¦103675.
\v 6 There¦103677 the¦103679 centurion¦103680 found¦103678 a ship¦103681 from¦103682 Alexandria¦103682 that was sailing¦103683 to¦103684 Italy¦103686, so he¦103687 boarded us¦103688 on that.
\p
\v 7 After many¦103693 days¦103695 of slow¦103696 and difficult¦103698 sailing¦103696, we came alongside¦103700 Cnidus¦103702 but¦103694 the¦103706 wind¦103707 wasn't helpful to us¦103705, so we sailed¦103708 across to Crete¦103710 and along¦103700 the cape of Salmone.
\v 8 We¦103717 sailed¦103715 along¦103715 it¦103716 with¦103713 difficulty¦103713 until we¦103717 came¦103717 to¦103718 a place¦103719 called¦103721 ‘Fair Havens’ which¦103724 was¦103728 near¦103725 the¦103727 city¦103727 of Lasea.
\p
\v 9 A lot of¦103740 time¦103732 had¦103746 now elapsed¦103733 and¦103734 the¦103738 voyage¦103739 was¦103747 starting to¦103746 become dangerous¦103737 because¦103740 we were already¦103736 heading into winter. Paul¦103749 advised¦103747 those¦103747 in charge,
\v 10 telling¦103750 them¦103751, “Men¦103752, I perceive¦103753 that¦103754 we're going¦103774 to¦103775 encounter a lot of damage¦103756 on this voyage¦103777, with¦103755 loss¦103759 not¦103760 only¦103761 of the¦103762 cargo¦103765 and¦103766 the¦103767 ship¦103768, but¦103757 also¦103770 our¦103773 lives¦103772.”
\v 11 But¦103783 the¦103778 centurion¦103780 was¦103788 persuaded¦103788 by the¦103784 ship¦103785 owner¦103785 and¦103783 the¦103781 captain¦103782, rather¦103787 than¦103789 by what Paul¦103793 had said.
\v 12 They¦103811 realised that this harbour wasn't suitable to winter in, so the majority suggested leaving there¦103807 with the expectation of¦103819 being¦103799 able¦103811 to get to Crete¦103819 to winter in the harbour at¦103814 Phoenix¦103815 (which was open to both the southwest¦103822 and¦103823 the northwest¦103825).
\s1 The severe storm at sea
\rem /s1 The Storm at Sea
\p
\v 13 When¦103828 a south¦103829 wind¦103829 started blowing¦103826 gently¦103826, they thought they could make it, so they pulled in the¦103837 anchor and¦103828 sailed¦103836 along¦103836 close to¦103833 Crete¦103838.
\v 14 However¦103851, a short while later¦103839 the¦103851 wind¦103847 turned to a violent north-easter which¦103849 forced us out to sea.
\v 15 The¦103854 ship¦103855 was driven by the¦103860 wind¦103861 and unable to tack to face into it, so the sailors gave¦103862 up¦103862 and just let it be¦103859 driven.
\v 16 As we passed a small island¦103864 called¦103868 Clauda¦103869, we got a brief break and¦103865 were¦103872 able¦103872 to¦103875 resecure the¦103876 lifeboat¦103877
\v 17 and then¦103887 bring it up¦103879 on board. Then¦103887 ropes were used to wrap around the ship in case it were to run¦103892 aground¦103892 into¦103889 the shallows¦103890 at Syrtis¦103891 and the¦103894 gear¦103895 was lowered¦103893,\f + \fr 27:17 \ft It's not certain what was meant here—it might have meant lowering the sails further to catch less wind or even lowering the mast, or else letting out the sea anchor to help keep the back of the ship facing the wind.\f* allowing the wind to drive¦103897 the ship along¦103897.
\v 18 We were¦103906 violently¦103898 tossed¦103901 around by the storm¦103901, and¦103900 the next¦103904 day¦103903 the crew started \add lightening the ship by\add* tipping \add some of the cargo\add* overboard.
\v 19 By the¦103908 third¦103909 day¦103909, the sailors themselves \add in desperation\add* were even throwing¦103916 some of¦103913 the ship's¦103914 gear overboard.
\v 20 We didn't see either the sun¦103919 or the stars¦103921 for¦103923 several more¦103924 days¦103925—it wasn't a minor storm¦103926 by any means—and¦103927 by then¦103918 any remaining hope¦103934 that¦103936 we had \add of staying alive\add* disappeared
\v 21 and¦103968 no one had eaten or drunk much¦103939 at all.
\p Then¦103940 Paul¦103947 stood¦103945 up in¦103948 the¦103961 middle of¦103950 them¦103950 and said¦103951, “Well, you should have listened to¦103957 me¦103957 after all and not¦103958 left Crete¦103962, so now we have this¦103967 damage¦103966 and loss¦103970.
\v 22 Nevertheless I think we can all¦103975 cheer¦103976 up¦103976 because¦103978 not¦103980 a single¦103980 life¦103979 will¦103982 be¦103982 lost—only¦103985 the¦103986 ship¦103987—
\v 23 because¦103989 last night¦103994 one of the messengers¦104005 of the \nd God¦103998\nd* that¦103992 I¦104000 serve¦104004 came and¦104003 stood¦103988 beside me¦103990
\v 24 and told¦104006 me not¦104007 to be¦104008 afraid¦104008 because I would indeed get to stand¦104013 before¦104013 Caesar¦104010 and that \nd God¦104019\nd* has¦104016 granted¦104016 your lives to me as well.
\v 25 So¦104025 cheer¦104026 up¦104026, fellows, because¦104029 I'm¦104028 certain that¦104032 \nd God¦104031\nd* will¦104034 carry out what was told to¦104035 me¦104040
\v 26 even though we'll run¦104048 aground¦104048 onto an island¦104042 first.”
\rem /s1 The Shipwreck
\p
\v 27 So it happened that in¦104057 the middle¦104061 of our fourteenth¦104051 night¦104063 of being¦104055 blown across the¦104058 Adriatic¦104059 Sea, the¦104065 sailors¦104066 suspected¦104064 that we were¦104064 nearing land¦104072.
\v 28 They¦104076 dropped a weighted line and measured the ocean depth at around¦104081 40 metres and then¦104074 a bit later it was down to 30 metres.
\v 29 Worrying that the ship might run¦104102 aground¦104102 on¦104097 rough¦104099 \add rocks\add*, they threw¦104106 out four¦104108 anchors¦104107 from¦104104 the stern¦104105 as they hoped that daylight would come quickly.
\v 30 Then¦104113 the¦104112 sailors¦104114 lowered¦104122 the¦104123 lifeboat¦104124 as¦104129 if planning to pull out¦104135 some anchors¦104133 from¦104130 the bow¦104131, but¦104113 really planning to desert the¦104119 ship¦104120.
\v 31 However Paul¦104138 told the¦104139 centurion¦104140 and¦104141 the¦104142 soldiers¦104143 that if¦104144 the sailors didn't¦104145 remain¦104147 with the¦104149 ship¦104150, everybody couldn't be sure to¦104153 be saved¦104153,
\v 32 so the¦104158 soldiers¦104159 cut¦104157 the¦104161 ropes¦104162 attaching the¦104163 lifeboat¦104164 and¦104165 allowed¦104166 it to¦104168 run¦104168 aground¦104168.
\p
\v 33 As they waited for the¦104178 daylight, Paul¦104179 encouraged¦104176 them all¦104180 to eat something, saying¦104183, “Today¦104185 is¦104186 our fourteenth¦104184 day¦104173 and¦104170 you've had¦104191 nothing¦104190 to eat. As we're waiting¦104187,
\v 34 I urge you to eat something. This¦104201 will¦104218 help you to have strength because¦104211 none of¦104200 you are going to drown.”
\v 35 Having¦104220 said¦104220 that, he¦104226 gave¦104226 thanks¦104226 to¦104235 \nd God¦104229\nd* in front¦104230 of everyone¦104231, and¦104221 breaking¦104233 some bread¦104225, started¦104234 eating¦104235 it¦104233.
\v 36 They¦104242 all¦104240 cheered¦104236 up and¦104237 also¦104241 started eating.
\v 37 There were¦104248 two¦104260 hundred¦104260 and¦104249 seventy-six¦104261 of them on the¦104257 ship¦104258,
\v 38 and¦104265 when they'd all eaten what they wanted, they lightened¦104268 the¦104269 ship¦104270 more by throwing¦104271 their wheat¦104273 into¦104274 the¦104275 sea¦104276.
\s1 The ship runs aground
\rem /s1 The Shipwreck
\p
\v 39 Once it became¦104280 light, they saw that¦104294 they were¦104296 close to shore¦104291 in a bay¦104286 but¦104278 didn't¦104283 recognise¦104285 the place. After some discussion, they decided to try to save the¦104303 ship¦104304
\v 40 so they cut¦104309 away¦104309 the¦104306 anchors¦104307 that were¦104310 holding them in¦104311 the¦104312 sea¦104313 and¦104305 untied the¦104318 rudder¦104319 \add handles\add*. Then¦104305 raising the¦104322 foresail¦104323, they let the wind¦104324 blow¦104325 them into the¦104328 shore¦104329.
\v 41 But¦104338 they¦104335 hit a bank with ocean on¦104340 both¦104334 sides of it and¦104338 the ship ran¦104335 aground¦104335. The¦104339 bow¦104342 of the ship was completely stuck¦104343 but¦104331 the¦104347 stern¦104349 was being¦104350 broken up by¦104352 the¦104353 force¦104354 of the ocean.
\p
\v 42 At this point the¦104357 soldiers¦104359 decided that¦104362 they¦104365 should¦104365 kill¦104365 the¦104363 prisoners¦104364 in case any of¦104357 them decided to swim¦104368 away¦104368 and¦104358 escape¦104369,
\v 43 but¦104372 the¦104371 centurion¦104373 wanted¦104374 to keep Paul¦104377 safe so he¦104384 vetoed that plan. He¦104384 commanded¦104384 those¦104380 who could¦104388 swim to jump off¦104391 and¦104386 swim in to shore,
\v 44 and for the others¦104407 to¦104418 grab \add floating\add* planks¦104405 or something off the¦104412 ship¦104413 \add that would float\add*, and so¦104415 everyone¦104403 got safely¦104418 onto the beach.
\c 28
\s1 On the island of Malta
\rem /s1 In Malta; Ashore on Malta; Paul on the Island of Malta
\p
\v 1 Once everyone was safe, we¦104426 discovered that¦104427 the¦104429 island¦104430 is¦104431 called¦104431 Malta.
\v 2 And¦104433 the¦104432 people there showed incredible kindness¦104440 to¦104441 us as they¦104447 welcomed us and lit¦104442 a fire¦104445, as it started raining again and was very cold¦104458.
\v 3 However when Paul¦104462 was helping to pick up sticks¦104463 and putting them¦104467 on the¦104472 fire¦104473, a viper¦104474 was driven out¦104480 by the¦104477 heat¦104478 and clamped¦104481 its jaws down¦104481 on his¦104485 hand¦104484.
\v 4 When¦104487 the islanders saw¦104488 the snake hanging¦104491 from¦104494 his hand¦104496 they¦104501 decided that¦104507 he must be¦104515 a murderer¦104503 who had survived the shipwreck but¦104487 now fate had finally decided his time was up.
\v 5 However, Paul just shook¦104521 the snake off¦104521 into¦104525 the¦104526 fire¦104527 and wasn't harmed.
\v 6 The locals were¦104533 waiting¦104547 for his arm to swell¦104538 up¦104538 or¦104539 for him¦104553 to fall¦104540 down¦104540 dead¦104542, but¦104532 after a while when nothing¦104550 happened they¦104557 changed¦104555 their¦104555 minds¦104555 and¦104548 decided that instead, he must be¦104561 a god¦104562.
\p
\v 7 The¦104565 area that¦104569 we had¦104579 beached in¦104563 belonged to¦104572 the leader¦104573 of¦104574 the island¦104575 named Publius¦104577. He welcomed us and¦104564 looked after us for three¦104581 days¦104582,
\v 8 but¦104587 while we were there, his father¦104589 got sick with¦104596 dysentery¦104594 and fever¦104592. Paul¦104601 went in¦104602 and placing his hands¦104608 on¦104606 him, prayed¦104604 for him and healed¦104610 him.
\v 9 As a result of that¦104619, anyone on the¦104617 whole island¦104624 who was sick came and¦104613 was cured
\v 10 and so the¦104640 people brought us many¦104633 gifts and when we left, they¦104639 supplied us with¦104633 provisions.
\s1 Sailing from Malta to Rome
\rem /s1 Arrival at Rome; From Malta to Rome; Paul Arrives at Rome
\p
\v 11 After¦104647 three¦104649 months¦104650 we¦104652 were¦104652 able to¦104661 leave on an¦104659 Alexandrian¦104659 ship¦104654 that had¦104655 wintered¦104655 at the¦104657 island¦104658. It¦104661 was decorated with¦104660 carvings of the twin¦104661 ‘gods’.
\v 12 We¦104666 stayed three¦104669 days¦104667 when we¦104666 arrived at¦104664 Syracuse¦104665
\v 13 and¦104677 then¦104677 we sailed on to Rhegium¦104676. The¦104683 next day¦104683 a south¦104682 wind¦104682 came¦104684 so we sailed on to Puteoli¦104686
\v 14 where¦104687 we found¦104688 some believers¦104689 and¦104698 they encouraged us to stay for seven¦104697 days¦104696. Then¦104698 we \add finally\add* got to Rome¦104703.
\v 15 The¦104706 believers¦104707 in Rome had heard¦104708 about¦104710 us, so they came¦104712 to meet¦104715 us at the Appius¦104720 Market¦104721 and the Three¦104723 Taverns¦104724. Paul¦104728 was encouraged to see¦104726 them and thanked¦104729 \nd God¦104731\nd*.
\s1 Paul preaches in Rome
\rem /s1 In Rome
\p
\v 16 When¦104735 we¦104736 got into Rome¦104740 proper, Paul¦104751 was¦104748 allowed to¦104738 live by¦104754 himself¦104756, with¦104757 just a soldier¦104761 guarding¦104759 him¦104760.
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches at Rome under Guard; Paul and Jewish Leaders in Rome
\p
\v 17 After¦104764 three¦104766 days¦104765, he called¦104767 together¦104767 the leaders¦104775 of¦104773 the Jews¦104774 \add in Rome\add*, and when they'd all gathered he spoke to them¦104782, “Men¦104785, brothers¦104786, I have done¦104790 nothing¦104788 against my people¦104792 or¦104793 against our ancestral¦104797 customs¦104795. I was¦104801 taken prisoner¦104798 in Yerushalem¦104800 and handed¦104801 over¦104801 to the Romans
\v 18 who¦104807, after examining¦104808 me, wanted¦104811 to¦104813 release¦104813 me because¦104814 I had¦104808 done nothing worthy of¦104814 execution.
\v 19 But¦104823 in defending myself against the Jews there, I felt compelled¦104826 to appeal¦104827 to Caesar¦104828, although I myself had¦104835 no complaint against the Jews.
\v 20 So¦104841 that¦104840 was why I called for you and¦104848 wanted to¦104847 address¦104849 you, because¦104851 I am¦104859 in¦104859 chains¦104857 for the sake of¦104855 Yisrael's¦104855 hope—\add the messiah\add*.”
\p
\v 21 “We¦104865 haven't received¦104872 any correspondence here about¦104869 you¦104871,” they¦104860 answered¦104864. “Nor¦104876 have¦104877 any of our brothers¦104880 \add from Yerushalem\add* arrived¦104877 here to¦104862 report¦104881 any crimes of yours.
\v 22 But¦104889 we would very much like to¦104893 hear¦104893 from¦104891 you¦104892 about your thinking¦104895, because we've certainly heard about this¦104901 new sect¦104900 because everyone¦104907 is¦104905 speaking¦104908 out against¦104908 it¦104908.”
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches in Rome
\p
\v 23 Then¦104910 they set a day¦104912 when they could visit him¦104916 where he was¦104922 staying and so that from¦104939 morning¦104948 to¦104949 evening¦104950 he could explain¦104922 about \nd God's¦104930\nd* kingdom¦104928 and show them¦104934 how Mosheh's¦104943 law¦104942 and the¦104945 prophets' writings spoke about \nd Yeshua¦104938\nd*.
\v 24 Afterwards some were¦104955 persuaded¦104955 while others¦104959 didn't find it believable.
\v 25 They¦104967 argued back and forth, and as they¦104967 were¦104967 about¦104988 to leave, Paul¦104970 shared this final¦104972 message¦104971, “The¦104969 holy¦104978 \nd spirit¦104976\nd* spoke¦104979 rightly¦104974 through Yeshayah¦104982 the¦104983 prophet¦104984 to your¦104988 ancestors¦104987, saying:
\q1
\v 26 Go to¦104997 these people¦104999 and¦105001 tell¦104994 them,
\q1 When you listen you will¦105004 hear¦105003 but not¦105016 understand¦105009,
\q1 and when you look you will see but not perceive¦105017 it.
\q1
\v 27 Because¦105020 these people's¦105024 minds have become¦105019 dull¦105019
\q2 and¦105026 they¦105031 can barely hear¦105044 with¦105027 their¦105027 ears¦105028
\q2 and they shut¦105036 their eyes¦105034,
